# Callouts

Setting: `ext_callouts`.

A block quote whose first line is a header `[!kind]` is a callout, as in
Obsidian.

``` example
> [!note] Backlinks
> A backlink is a link seen from its target.
.
<div class="callout" data-callout="note">
<div class="callout-title">Backlinks</div>
<div class="callout-content">
<p>A backlink is a link seen from its target.</p>
</div>
</div>
```

## The header

The header is tested on the line that opens a quote, and only there. After
the quote prefix it is:

1. `[!`;
2. a kind: one or more ASCII letters, digits, `-` or `_`;
3. `]`;
4. optionally a fold marker, `+` or `-`;
5. then the end of the line, or a space or tab and the title.

The kind is kept as written. The title is the rest of the line, parsed as
inline content.

``` example
> [!warning]- Do *not* rename
> The fold marker makes it a `details` element.
.
<details class="callout" data-callout="warning">
<summary class="callout-title">Do <strong>not</strong> rename</summary>
<div class="callout-content">
<p>The fold marker makes it a <code>details</code> element.</p>
</div>
</details>
```

A later line of an open quote is not tested.

``` example
> a
> [!note] T
.
<blockquote>
<p>a
[!note] T</p>
</blockquote>
```

## What is not a header

Anything else after the `]` leaves the line as djot reads it. This keeps
links, spans and reference definitions that begin with `[!`.

``` example
> [!note](x)
.
<blockquote>
<p><a href="x">!note</a></p>
</blockquote>
```

``` example
> [!no te] T
.
<blockquote>
<p>[!no te] T</p>
</blockquote>
```

## The body

The following lines of the quote are the body, parsed as blocks. No blank
line is needed after the header. A line without `>` directly after the
header closes the callout, because the header opens no paragraph for it to
continue.

``` example
> [!note] T
after
.
<div class="callout" data-callout="note">
<div class="callout-title">T</div>
<div class="callout-content">
</div>
</div>
<p>after</p>
```

Inside the body, a paragraph continues onto a line without `>` as in any
quote.

``` example
> [!note] T
> para
lazy
.
<div class="callout" data-callout="note">
<div class="callout-title">T</div>
<div class="callout-content">
<p>para
lazy</p>
</div>
</div>
```

## Output

Obsidian's classes. `data-callout` holds the kind. With a fold marker the
outer element is `details`, open for `+` and closed for `-`. The tree keeps
a node of its own, with the kind, the fold marker, the title and the body.

